# Forgiveness
#
# The question this program asks is whether Bob can forgive Alice without
# forgetting what she did.
#
# In an ordinary language you would model this with a flag, and forgiving would
# mean setting it back.  In Catharsis you cannot, because the memory is not a
# record of the betrayal -- it is a live participant in it.  Every tick, Bob's
# memory pushes its own charge back into his bond with Alice, and while it is
# salient it holds a *ceiling* on how much he is able to trust her again.
#
# `forgive` disarms the memory: it stops the memory pushing anger and resentment.
# It does not delete it, it does not lift the ceiling, and it does not touch the
# grief -- forgiveness costs a little more grief, in fact, because letting go of
# the grievance means admitting the loss.
#
# Run it and compare the numbers at the end:
#
#     trust      recovers, but never past the ceiling the memory imposes
#     grief      stays
#     the memory stays, flagged forgiven, still pushing grief and sadness

alice = agent
bob = agent

love alice bob 0.8
love bob alice 0.8
trust bob alice 0.9

betray alice bob

grief bob
anger bob alice
doubt bob alice

tick 6

# Alice tries. Whether an apology lands at all depends on what Bob is holding:
# resentment and doubt absorb it, love and hope let it through.
apology alice bob
hope bob alice

tick 4

forgive bob alice
trust bob alice

tick 12

observe bob

# The memory is still there, and still has weight.
recall bob "alice betrayed bob"
